# SQL security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic security rules to injection
safety and access control for `.sql` files across Postgres, MySQL, and
generic SQL engines. Applies only to `*.sql` files — parameterized-query
enforcement in application code (the driver/ORM call site) is covered by
that language's own stack pack; this file covers the `.sql` side (stored
procedures, functions, views) and the shared vocabulary both sides share.

## Injection

- Never build a query by concatenating or string-formatting a
  user-influenced value into SQL text — this applies inside a stored
  procedure or function body exactly as it does in application code: a
  dynamic `EXECUTE`/`EXEC` built from concatenated input is injectable
  even though the vulnerable code lives in the database, not the app.
- Postgres `EXECUTE`/`format()` inside `PL/pgSQL`: use `format('...%I...',
  identifier)` for identifiers (table/column names that must be dynamic)
  and `format('...%L...', value)` or a parameterized `USING` clause for
  values — `%I`/`%L` quote-escape correctly; plain string concatenation
  of either does not.
- MySQL stored procedures: build dynamic SQL with `PREPARE stmt FROM
  @sql; EXECUTE stmt USING @param;` — bind user-influenced values through
  `USING`, never interpolate them into `@sql` itself before `PREPARE`.
- An ORM or query builder's raw-SQL escape hatch (a `.raw()`, `.exec()`,
  or template-literal query method) carries the same rule as a
  hand-written query: pass user-influenced values through its
  placeholder/parameter API, never through string interpolation into the
  raw SQL text, even when the surrounding call looks like it's "just
  building a string for convenience."
- A dynamic identifier (a table or column name chosen at runtime, common
  in multi-tenant schema-per-tenant designs or admin tooling) cannot go
  through a value placeholder at all — placeholders bind values, not
  identifiers. Validate it against an allowlist of known-safe identifiers
  before using it, never trust it as free text even when it "came from
  our own config."

## Access control

- Grant the narrowest role/privilege a connection actually needs — an
  application's runtime database user should not hold `DROP`/`CREATE`/
  superuser privileges it never uses; a migration runner's credentials
  (which do need DDL rights) should be separate from the application's
  runtime connection.
- Use row-level security (Postgres `CREATE POLICY` on a table with `ROW
  LEVEL SECURITY` enabled) or an equivalent application-enforced tenant
  filter consistently — do not rely on every query remembering to add
  `WHERE tenant_id = ?` by convention alone when the engine can enforce it
  structurally.
- A view or stored procedure that runs with elevated privileges
  (Postgres `SECURITY DEFINER`, a MySQL `DEFINER`-owned routine) is a
  privilege-escalation surface if it accepts unvalidated input that
  influences what it reads or writes — treat its parameters with the same
  injection discipline as a top-level query, since callers with lower
  privileges can reach the elevated code path through it.

## Data exposure

- Never `SELECT *` a table containing a sensitive column (password hash,
  API token, PII) into a result set a caller with less trust than the
  query's own privilege level will see — select named columns so adding a
  sensitive column later can't leak silently into an existing query.
- Encrypt or hash sensitive columns before they reach storage
  (`pgcrypto`'s `crypt()`/`digest()` on Postgres, an application-layer
  hash before insert) rather than relying on transport encryption alone —
  a compromised backup or replica exposes plaintext columns even when
  every connection to the primary was encrypted.
